4.1 Single keys and the prefix
==============================

While the buffer is read-only, which it is by default
(‘bltzread-make-read-only’), single keys are bound without a modifier.
They are not bound in a buffer you can edit, so typing is never in the
way.  Put the cursor on a word and press:

‘5’
     Set that level (the digits ‘1’ to ‘5’).
‘+’ and ‘-’
     Raise or lower the level by one, within 1 to 5; they never wrap.
     Give a count to step further.
‘i’
     Ignore the word.  On an ignored word, stop ignoring it and bring
     back its old level.
‘u’
     Undo the last bltzread change even when other edits came in
     between.  Ordinary ‘undo’ and ‘undo-redo’ also take back and
     restore bltzread changes (level, ignore, note, lookup, finished
     session) in order with everything else, although the buffer is
     read-only.
‘c’
     Close the card.  It returns when you move to another word.
‘d’ and ‘D’
     Show the next or previous dictionary entry when a word has several.
‘l’
     Look the word up in the dictionary (also a double click).  A single
     click opens the card even when the cursor was on the word already.
‘N’
     Write yourself a note about the word.  The card shows it.
‘]’ and ‘[’
     Jump to the next or previous word that is still unknown.  A count
     jumps further; a negative count goes the other way.  If fewer
     remain bltzread moves as far as it can and says how far.

   All of these also work after the prefix ‘bltzread-prefix-key’
(default ‘s’), for example ‘s 3’, and the prefix gives the rarer
commands too:

‘s f’
     ‘bltzread-finish-session’, see _Reading sessions_.
‘s k’
     ‘bltzread-mark-range-known’, mark every word in the range you pick
     as known.
‘s r’
     ‘bltzread-review’, the review list.
‘s m’
     ‘bltzread-range-change-method’, change how you pick the range.
‘s <’ and ‘s >’
     Set the start and the end of the range yourself.
‘s a’
     ‘bltzread-annotate’, read the words again and paint them.
‘s L’
     ‘bltzread-set-language’, change the language of the buffer.
‘.’ (also ‘s .’)
     ‘bltzread-popup-at-point’, show the card for the word at point.

   ‘M-x describe-keymap RET bltzread-command-map’ lists them.  ‘M-x
bltzread-avy-unknown’ (needs the avy package) tags every unknown word in
view with a hint; type one to go to that word and open its card.  It has
no key by default.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Changing the keys,  Prev: Single keys and the prefix,  Up: Keys

4.2 Changing the keys
=====================

The keymap is ‘bltzread-mode-map’.  The prefix has its own option,
‘bltzread-prefix-key’ (nil for none); changing it in Customize rebinds
it at once.  To bind further keys use ‘keymap-set’.  To make a key work
only while the buffer is read-only, as the built-in ones do, wrap the
command with ‘bltzread-when-read-only’:

     (keymap-set bltzread-mode-map "x" (bltzread-when-read-only #'bltzread-ignore))
     (keymap-unset bltzread-mode-map "N" t)

   The mouse gestures are options: ‘bltzread-mouse-lookup-key’ (default
a double click) and ‘bltzread-mouse-popup-key’ (default a click).
Either can be nil.  They are applied when set through Customize or
‘setopt’; if you use ‘setq’, run ‘bltzread--install-mouse-keys’
afterwards.

   The keys listed in the card's last row come from the real keymaps
(see _The card_).


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The card,  Next: The dictionary,  Prev: Keys,  Up: Top

5 The card
**********

When the cursor rests on a word for a moment (‘bltzread-popup-delay’) a
small card appears.  It shows the word, its level, how many clean reads
it has, your note, and the dictionary entry.  In a graphical Emacs the
card floats above the word, always above and never below: if there is no
room the window is scrolled to make some (‘bltzread-popup-make-room’).
The card follows the word when the text scrolls
(‘bltzread-popup-on-scroll’).  It disappears when you switch buffer,
window or frame.  After you set a level it hides, and returns when you
leave the word and come back, or with ‘.’ after the prefix.  By default
(‘bltzread-popup-only-on-motion’) a card opens only after a command
moved the cursor onto a word, so text scrolled for you, for example by
subtle, neither opens cards nor counts lookups.  In a terminal the card
is shown in a window at the bottom, or in the echo area, as
‘bltzread-popup-style’ says.

   Looking a word up with ‘l’ shows a longer entry.  Lookups count once
per word per session.  Merely passing over a word is not a lookup: with
the default ‘bltzread-lookup-counts’ (‘command’) only ‘l’ counts.  Set
it to ‘interaction’ to let the entry keys and ‘.’ count too, or to
‘popup’ to count a card that stays on a word for ‘bltzread-lookup-dwell’
seconds.

   The card is built from rows.  Which rows, and in which order, is the
list ‘bltzread-popup-row-functions’ (see _Extending bltzread_).  The
last row shows the keys of the commands in ‘bltzread-popup-hints’ as key
caps.  A command without a key is left out, and the level commands are
left out in no-colors mode.  The keys are looked up at the time, so
rebinding them is reflected at once.  Only about ‘bltzread-popup-key’
characters of keys are shown for each command (the digits are shown as
‘1-5’).

   The window of the box can be placed with
‘bltzread-popup-display-action’ (a ‘display-buffer’ action; the existing
window is reused) and its frame with ‘bltzread-popup-frame-parameters’
(your entries win over the defaults).  ‘bltzread-popup-width’ is the
width in columns and is limited to the window.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The dictionary,  Next: Reading sessions,  Prev: The card,  Up: Top

6 The dictionary
****************

bltzread uses sdcv, a command-line dictionary program that reads
StarDict dictionaries.  You tell bltzread where your dictionaries are
and which to use for each language.  If sdcv or the dictionary is
missing the card simply has no entry; nothing breaks, and an error of
the lookup is reported in the echo area and not remembered, so the next
lookup tries again.

   A dictionary can be named by its exact name, its folder name (‘dpd’),
or any part of the name that picks out only one.  A language with no
entry in ‘bltzread-sdcv-dictionaries’ searches no dictionary at all (see
‘bltzread-sdcv-unlisted-languages’), so Slovene entries never turn up in
Pali.  bltzread warns once when a configured name matches nothing.  ‘M-x
bltzread-dict-diagnose’ shows the program, the folder, every dictionary
found and what each language uses, and runs a test lookup.
‘bltzread-sdcv-extra-args’ are added to every call.

   Some big dictionaries, such as the Digital Pāḷi Dictionary, have an
index that is not in the order sdcv expects, so exact lookups miss
almost every word.  ‘tools/sdcv-fix-index.py SRC OUT’ writes a fixed
copy and leaves the original alone.  Point
‘bltzread-sdcv-data-dir-alist’ at a folder holding the copy.

   You can replace sdcv with your own function
(‘bltzread-dictionary-function’, see _Extending bltzread_).

* Menu:

* Short dictionary entries::


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Short dictionary entries,  Up: The dictionary

6.1 Short dictionary entries
============================

The card can show only the part of an entry you care about.  bltzread
knows nothing about any particular dictionary: ‘bltzread-dict-filters’
says, per dictionary (the key is its name) or per language (the key is
the code), which pieces to show.  A spec is one of:

   • A regexp string.  Every match in the entry gives one line: the text
     of its first group, or the whole match.
   • A plist ‘(:class "gloss")’ taking the text just after each element
     with that class (a list for several classes).  More keys: ‘:split "
     · "’ cuts each piece into lines, ‘:max 5’ keeps the first five,
     ‘:join ", "’ puts the lines on one line.
   • A function taking the entry text, for anything else.

   Tags and entities are removed and repeated lines dropped.  The
headword and the dictionary name are not repeated.  If a spec finds
nothing the card falls back to the whole entry on purpose.  The lookup
key still shows the whole entry.

   Some entries only point to another word ("see X"), so the filter
finds nothing.  Add ‘:follow t’ to the spec and bltzread follows the
‘bword://’ links of the entry, looks the target up in the same
dictionary, and shows "→ target" with the target's short form.
‘:follow’ can also be a regexp whose first group is the word, or a
function returning a list of words.  ‘bltzread-dict-follow-depth’
(default 3) limits how many links in a row are followed.  Example:
‘("pol" :regexp "class’\"trans\">\[^<]*\" :max 6 :follow t)=.  Entries
are cached with the filter; run ‘M-x bltzread-dict-clear-cache’ after
changing a spec.  The tutorial walks through writing one.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Reading sessions,  Next: No-colors mode,  Prev: The dictionary,  Up: Top

7 Reading sessions
******************

You do not have to set every level by hand.  bltzread also promotes
words as you read:

  1. You read part of the text.
  2. You tell bltzread which part you read.  This is a "finish": press
     ‘s f’, or answer the question when you close the buffer.
  3. Every word in that part that you did not look up gets one "clean
     read".
  4. When a word has enough clean reads it moves up one level
     (‘bltzread-promotion-thresholds’: how many it needs at each level).
  5. A word you looked up during the session loses its clean reads and
     keeps its level (‘bltzread-lookup-effect’ can also demote it).

   A level you set by hand always wins over this automatic climbing.
‘C-u s f’ finishes and marks everything in the range as known at once.

   When the buffer is closed without a finish, bltzread asks whether to
record your reading (‘bltzread-prompt-on-close’), but only if enough
text was passed (‘bltzread-min-chars-for-prompt’).  A program that
closes buffers can call ‘bltzread-offer-record’ first; it asks once, and
the question is not repeated on close.

* Menu:

* Telling bltzread what you read::


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Telling bltzread what you read,  Up: Reading sessions

7.1 Telling bltzread what you read
==================================

The way is chosen by ‘bltzread-range-method’; you can try them in ‘M-x
bltzread-range-playground’.

farthest
     bltzread proposes everything from where you started to the farthest
     place you reached.  By default that place is the bottom of the text
     on screen.  With ‘bltzread-range-farthest-source’ set to ‘point’ or
     to a function returning a position, it is whatever that returns, so
     only text that was played counts.  ‘bltzread-range-start-function’
     does the same for the start.
points
     You mark a start and an end yourself with ‘s <’ and ‘s >’.
paragraphs
     Paragraph numbers appear in the margin; you type from and to.
lines
     The same, using line numbers.

   After the range is counted, bltzread may move your bookmark to the
end of it (‘bltzread-bookmark-policy’,
‘bltzread-bookmark-name-function’).  ‘bltzread-session-confirm’ says
whether to confirm the range first.

   In nov.el, turning a page replaces the text, so bltzread paints the
new page and forgets the old range.  Finish the session before you turn
the page if you want that reading counted.  Closing the buffer also
asks.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: No-colors mode,  Next: The review list,  Prev: Reading sessions,  Up: Top

8 No-colors mode
****************

Set ‘bltzread-no-colors’ (per buffer with ‘setq-local’, or ‘M-x
bltzread-toggle-colors’) to show no level colours and to disable setting
levels by hand.  The card stays, without the level unless
‘bltzread-no-colors-show-level’ is set.  When you finish or close, the
words you did not look up still get their clean reads and progress as
usual.  To make a "quiet mode" with only the card, see the recipes in
_Extending bltzread_.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The review list,  Next: Your skip lists,  Prev: No-colors mode,  Up: Top

9 The review list
*****************

Words that reach level 5 are not put on your skip list straight away
unless you ask (‘bltzread-known-write-policy’).  They wait in a queue.
‘M-x bltzread-review’ (or ‘s r’) shows it; its keys are listed in the
header of the list:

‘a’
     Add this word (every word starts as add).
‘d’
     Decide later.  It stays in the queue.
‘r’
     Reject.  The word drops back to ‘bltzread-review-reject-level’
     (default 4).
‘A’
     Mark every word add.
‘x’
     Apply: write the added words to the skip list (after
     ‘bltzread-review-confirm’).

   Words that reached level 5 by reading exposure rather than by hand
are listed first, highlighted with the face ‘bltzread-review-exposure’.
bltzread makes a ‘.bak’ copy of the list file before it writes.  The
hook ‘bltzread-review-after-apply-hook’ runs afterwards.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Your skip lists,  Next: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Prev: The review list,  Up: Top

10 Your skip lists
******************

bltzr has two skip lists: exact words (only the spelling you list) and
word families (a basic word and all its forms).  By default bltzread
adds the exact spelling you read to the exact-words list.  You can
choose word families instead, or be asked each time, and set this per
language (‘bltzread-list-target’, ‘bltzread-list-target-alist’).

   bltzread finds the files in your bltzr settings file
(‘bltzread-bltzr-config-file’, by default ‘~/.config/bltzr/bltzr.toml’).
You can name the files yourself with ‘bltzread-skip-exact-words-files’
and ‘bltzread-skip-word-families-files’.  Until you choose a
‘bltzread-known-write-policy’ (‘review’ or ‘immediate’), bltzread acts
as ‘review’ and warns once.

   The code that writes the words is ‘bltzread-known-writer-function’,
which you can replace.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Next: Options,  Prev: Your skip lists,  Up: Top

11 Where bltzread keeps its data
********************************

One file per language, in ‘bltzread-state-directory’ (by default a
folder named ‘bltzread’ in your Emacs directory).  It holds levels,
clean-read counts, notes, the queue and a short history.  Files are
plain text and saved a few seconds after a change.  A file that cannot
be read is not overwritten: it is copied to ‘NAME.corrupt’ and you are
warned.


