15.2.1 Step 1. Get some text
----------------------------

Open any text file in the language you are learning, or copy a paragraph
and run ‘M-x bltzread-read-text’.  The second is the easiest: it opens a
nicely spaced page.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 2 Turn the mode on,  Next: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Prev: Step 1 Get some text,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.2 Step 2. Turn the mode on
-------------------------------

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’.  If asked, type the three-letter language code,
for example ‘slv’, and press Enter.

   You should see: after a moment, words are coloured.  Strong yellow
means new.  Words on your skip list have no colour at all.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Next: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Prev: Step 2 Turn the mode on,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.3 Step 3. Rest on a word
-----------------------------

Move the cursor onto a coloured word and wait half a second.  A small
card appears with the word, its level and, if you set up a dictionary,
its meaning.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Next: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Prev: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.4 Step 4. Mark what you know
---------------------------------

With the cursor on a word, press ‘5’ with a digit: ‘5’ if you know it
perfectly.  The colour disappears.  Press ‘1’ to make it new again.
Press ‘3’ to say "familiar".

   Press ‘]’ to jump to the next word you do not know yet.  Press ‘[’ to
go back.  (‘s n’ and ‘s p’ do the same.)  Put a count first to jump
further.

   A level covers every form of the word, so ‘3’ on _hiše_ also sets
_hiša_.  Made a mistake?  Press ‘u’, or the ordinary undo key, as often
as you like; redo works too.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Next: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Prev: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.5 Step 5. Ignore a name
----------------------------

Put the cursor on a name and press ‘i’.  It turns grey and bltzread will
stop counting it.  Press it again to bring the word back.  If a card is
in your way, press ‘c’ to close it; ‘d’ shows the next dictionary entry
when there are several.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Next: Step 7 Leave a note,  Prev: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.6 Step 6. Look a word up properly
--------------------------------------

Press ‘l’ on a word, or double-click it.  A longer entry appears.
Looking a word up tells bltzread you did not know it: it restarts that
word's progress.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 7 Leave a note,  Next: Step 8 Turn it off,  Prev: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.7 Step 7. Leave a note
---------------------------

Press ‘N’, type your own hint, press Enter.  Next time the card shows
it.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Step 8 Turn it off,  Next: Jump to unknown words with avy,  Prev: Step 7 Leave a note,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.8 Step 8. Turn it off
--------------------------

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’ again.  The colours go and the buffer is
editable.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Jump to unknown words with avy,  Prev: Step 8 Turn it off,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

15.2.9 Jump to unknown words with avy
-------------------------------------

If you have the ‘avy’ package, run ‘M-x bltzread-avy-unknown’.  Every
unknown word on screen gets a hint; type it to go to that word and open
its card.  Escape or ‘C-g’ removes the hints and continues as
‘avy-goto-char-timer’.  Bind it to a key you like.


